#pagebreak()

= Introduction

The Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes (RANS) Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations central to aircraft design remain computationally expensive; machine-learning surrogates are an attractive way to make this analysis fast enough for iterative design, provided the model can be trusted to generalize rather than merely interpolate. This report describes a machine learning challenge, designed during an internship at LISN in collaboration with ONERA and hosted on Codabench @codabench (platform details in @app_platform), that targets prediction of the non-dimensional wall density field $rho\/rho_infinity$ over the NASA/Boeing Common Research Model (CRM) @crm_database @peter2025onera from 468 RANS simulations (260,774 surface points each), given the aircraft geometry and three flow conditions: freestream Mach number $M_infinity$, angle of attack $alpha$, and stagnation pressure $Pi$ (full dataset and physics background in @app_dataset).

The central design question is whether a model has learned the underlying physics rather than the training distribution. Two choices address this directly: a train/test split that separately evaluates interpolation and extrapolation (@sec_split), and a primary metric that targets the *shape* of the error distribution rather than a single aggregate number (@sec_metrics). Two main baselines illustrate promising directions for participants: POD+Gaussian-Process (POD+GP) and a full-field MLP trained on a differentiable relaxation of the metric ($"MLP"_"KLW"$); these and four other baselines are compared in @sec_results on a rebalanced Mach-stratified split.

== Related Work <sec_related>

Existing benchmarks for aerodynamic ML surrogates typically evaluate models purely in-distribution. AirfRANS @airfrans2022, the closest precedent, does separate in-distribution and out-of-distribution test conditions (Reynolds-number and angle-of-attack extrapolation regimes), which motivated the two-phase structure adopted here, and evaluates with Spearman correlation and root mean squared error. This challenge differs by targeting a 3-dimensional full-surface scalar field rather than a 2D airfoil, and by scoring the *shape* of the error distribution via a Kullback-Leibler divergence to a reference distribution rather than a first- or second-moment aggregate; the general framing of intrinsic versus learnable difficulty follows @pavao2025ai.

= Methods

== Train/Test Split Design <sec_split>

A challenge's difficulty decomposes into an intrinsic part (data noise and flaws) and a learnable part (recoverable by any model) @pavao2025ai; the goal is a split that maximizes the ratio of the latter to the former. We test both interpolation and extrapolation, since a model that generalizes to unseen flow conditions must have captured genuine structure rather than exploited local smoothness, particularly given that the flow-condition distribution clusters densely in several regions (e.g. $M_infinity in [0.8, 0.9]$), where locality-based methods such as KNN can otherwise score deceptively well on interpolation splits (see @app_results). Analysis of $rho\/rho_infinity$ against each flow-condition axis showed the distribution's parameters are clearly correlated with $M_infinity$ but not with $alpha$ or $Pi$, motivating a Mach-stratified split (@split_v3_table): the excluded interpolation band (Phase 1) sits inside the dense training cluster, while the extrapolation band (Phase 2) sits at the distribution's extremes, entirely outside the training range.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, center, center, center),
    stroke: 0.4pt,
    table.header[*Split*][*Mach numbers*][*\# simulations*][*\% of 468*],
    [Train], [0.50, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80, 0.82, 0.85, 0.88, 0.90, 0.93], [324], [69.2%],
    [Phase 1 (interp.)], [0.84, 0.86], [72], [15.4%],
    [Phase 2 (extrap.)], [0.30, 0.96], [72], [15.4%],
  ),
  caption: [Train/test split employed in this challenge.]
) <split_v3_table>

== Evaluation Metrics <sec_metrics>

Pearson's $R^2$ and a worst-case relative MAE (wrMAE) are used as secondary metrics, but neither is sensitive to *localized, structured* errors: a model can score near-perfectly overall while systematically mispredicting a small, physically important region (e.g. the pylon), since both metrics average over all points (full formulas in @app_metrics). @bimodal_fig_body illustrates the failure mode this misses: a dominant peak centered at 0 with a secondary peak offset from zero, revealing a systematic, localized bias that averaging-based metrics do not detect.

#figure(
  image("figures/bimodal.png", width: 62%),
  caption: [An error distribution that has to be penalized severely: a dominant peak at 0 plus a secondary, offset peak revealing a localized bias.]
) <bimodal_fig_body>

The primary metric instead targets the *shape* of the per-simulation residual distribution via a Kullback-Leibler divergence to a centered reference Gaussian $cal(N)(0, 0.01macron(rho))$, computed separately per aircraft component (wing, pylon, fuselage, nacelle) and combined with weights $w_"wing"=w_"pylon"=0.3$, $w_"fuselage"=w_"nacelle"=0.2$ that emphasize the two hardest-to-predict components:

$ "KL"_i^w = D_"KL" (P_i^w || cal(N)(0, 0.01 macron(rho)_i)), quad overline("KL"^w) = 1/N sum_(i=1)^N "KL"_i^w $

$overline("KL"^w)$ (denoted *mean_KL*) is the leaderboard's primary, rank-determining metric (lower is better). Because the histogram underlying it is not differentiable, $"MLP"_"KLW"$ (@sec_baselines) is instead trained on a soft-binned relaxation of the same objective, replacing the hard bin assignment with a softmax over bin centers; the full derivation, including the exact PyTorch tensor shapes of the resulting rank-3 backpropagation, is given in @klw_loss.

= Baselines <sec_baselines>

*POD+GP.* Training density fields are reduced via Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD, equivalently PCA) to $k=55$ retained modes; one independent Gaussian Process (Matérn, $nu=1.5$) is fit per mode, mapping $(M_infinity, alpha, Pi)$ to that mode's coefficient, and the field is reconstructed via the *exact linear* POD decoder $hat(rho) = macron(rho) + sum_(m=1)^k z_m phi_m$ <pod_decoder>. A Gaussian Process is chosen over a fixed interpolator precisely because the kernel it is built on decides which functions are plausible a priori: an RBF/Matérn kernel forces nearby flow conditions to covary and yields smooth, physically plausible mode coefficients, whereas an unconstrained interpolator has no such prior (@gp_kernel_fig_body). Hyperparameters were tuned via a 181-trial Optuna search (@app_baselines); a full primer on Gaussian Processes and their link to this decoder is given in @app_gp.

#figure(
  image("figures/gp_kernel_comparison.png", width: 92%),
  caption: [Samples from the same prior $f tilde.op cal(N)(0, K)$ under two kernels: identity (left, uncorrelated noise) versus RBF (right, smooth, plausible functions). POD+GP's per-mode GPs use a Matérn kernel, closely related to the RBF case.]
) <gp_kernel_fig_body>

*$"MLP"_"KLW"$.* A full-field MLP (hidden layers 128, 256, 512) trained directly on the differentiable KL relaxation of @klw_loss, serving as a direction for participants to explore deep architectures rather than as a tuned, final model.

*Other baselines* (full descriptions and tuning in @app_baselines):

- A *global KNN regressor* ($k=5$ nearest neighbors on the scaled flow conditions alone, geometry not used at all) predicts the entire field as the average of the 5 nearest training simulations. It scores deceptively well on interpolation by proximity ($R^2=0.9824$ on Phase 1) but collapses on extrapolation, since it never learns the actual dependence on $(M_infinity, alpha, Pi)$, only how to look up the closest match.
- A *mean predictor* simply predicts the global training mean, as a sanity floor every other baseline should beat.
- *IsoMap+RBF*, the best-performing model from @peter2025onera, embeds fields into a 3D latent space via IsoMap and maps flow conditions to that space with an RBF interpolator; reconstruction backmaps via an inverse-distance-weighted average of the $k$ nearest training fields in latent space, a convex combination that therefore cannot exceed the training range.
- A *literature global MLP* @peter2025onera (hidden layers 75, 120, 1226, 16490), trained with plain MSE rather than a KL-aware loss; the top-ranked architecture in the predecessor challenge, evaluated here only on the Margaret cluster due to Codabench resource limits.

= Results and Discussion <sec_results>

@v3_results_table reports every baseline on both phases of the rebalanced split; *mean_KL* is the rank-determining metric (lower is better). All values are live Codabench scores except Global MLP, evaluated only on the Margaret Inria supercomputer.

